  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Inverse and Implicit Function Theorems [S+N]\n\nTwo structural theorems that turn the local linear approximation (Jacobian) from the previous notebook into existence statements about *real* functions.\n\n**Inverse function theorem.** Let $f : U \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ be $C^1$ on an open set $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$. If $J_f(x_0)$ is invertible, then there's a neighbourhood $V$ of $x_0$ on which $f$ is a $C^1$ bijection onto its image, and the inverse $f^{-1}$ is $C^1$ with Jacobian $J_{f^{-1}}(f(x_0)) = J_f(x_0)^{-1}$.\n\n**Implicit function theorem.** Let $F : \\mathbb{R}^n \\times \\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ be $C^1$. Suppose $F(x_0, y_0) = 0$ and the partial Jacobian $\\partial F/\\partial y$ at $(x_0, y_0)$ is invertible. Then near $x_0$ there's a unique $C^1$ function $y = g(x)$ with $g(x_0) = y_0$ and $F(x, g(x)) = 0$. The Jacobian of $g$ is\n\n$$J_g(x_0) = -\\left(\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial y}\\right)^{-1} \\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nIn one breath: *if the linear approximation is invertible, the nonlinear thing is locally invertible too.*\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 13,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Inverse function theorem — polar coordinates\n\nTake $f(r, \\theta) = (r \\cos\\theta, r \\sin\\theta)$ — the polar-to-Cartesian map. Compute the Jacobian, check invertibility, and find $f^{-1}$ explicitly.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_polar : [r * cos(theta), r * sin(theta)]$\nJ_polar : genmatrix(lambda([i, j], diff(f_polar[i], [r, theta][j])), 2, 2)$\nprint(\"J_f =\")$ J_polar;\nprint(\"det(J_f) = r·cos²θ + r·sin²θ =\")$\nratsimp(determinant(J_polar));",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "J_f =\nmatrix([cos(theta),-(r*sin(theta))],[sin(theta),r*cos(theta)])\ndet(J_f) = r·cos²θ + r·sin²θ ="
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "r\\,\\sin ^2\\theta+r\\,\\cos ^2\\theta"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 14,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "$\\det J_f = r$ — invertible everywhere except at the origin ($r = 0$). At any $r > 0$, the inverse function theorem applies and $f$ is locally invertible.\n\nThe local inverse, of course, is the familiar Cartesian-to-polar map: $r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$, $\\theta = \\mathrm{atan2}(y, x)$. By the theorem, its Jacobian at $(x_0, y_0)$ is $J_f^{-1}$ at the corresponding $(r_0, \\theta_0)$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "J_inv_polar : invert(J_polar)$\nprint(\"J_{f^{-1}} = J_f^{-1} =\")$\nratsimp(J_inv_polar);\nprint(\"(In Cartesian coordinates these are ∂r/∂x, ∂r/∂y, ∂θ/∂x, ∂θ/∂y)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "J_{f^{-1}} = J_f^{-1} =\nmatrix([cos(theta)/(sin(theta)^2+cos(theta)^2),\n        sin(theta)/(sin(theta)^2+cos(theta)^2)],\n       [-(sin(theta)/(r*sin(theta)^2+r*cos(theta)^2)),\n        cos(theta)/(r*sin(theta)^2+r*cos(theta)^2)])\n(In Cartesian coordinates these are ∂r/∂x, ∂r/∂y, ∂θ/∂x, ∂θ/∂y)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "**Failure at the origin.** $\\det J_f = 0$ at $r = 0$ — and indeed the polar map is *not* invertible there: every $(0, \\theta)$ maps to the same point $(0, 0)$.\n\n## Implicit function theorem — circle as graph [S+N]\n\nConsider $F(x, y) = x^2 + y^2 - 1$. The level set $F = 0$ is the unit circle. Locally, when can we solve for $y$ as a function of $x$?\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial y} = 2y.$$\n\nThis is invertible (non-zero) **except where $y = 0$** — i.e., at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$. Away from those two points, the implicit function theorem says the circle is locally the graph of $y(x)$, with\n\n$$y'(x) = -\\frac{\\partial F/\\partial x}{\\partial F/\\partial y} = -\\frac{2x}{2y} = -\\frac{x}{y}.$$\n\nAt $(\\pm 1, 0)$, the circle is vertical — no function $y(x)$ exists locally there (you'd need $x(y)$ instead).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "F_circle : x^2 + y^2 - 1$\ndF_dx : diff(F_circle, x)$\ndF_dy : diff(F_circle, y)$\ny_prime : -dF_dx / dF_dy$\nprint(\"y'(x) =\")$\nratsimp(y_prime);\nprint(\"At (0.6, 0.8): y'(x) =\", float(subst([x=0.6, y=0.8], y_prime)))$\nprint(\"Slope from explicit formula  d/dx √(1-x²) at x=0.6 =\")$\nprint(\"  \", float(subst(x=0.6, diff(sqrt(1 - x^2), x))))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 16,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "y'(x) =\n-(x/y)\nAt (0.6, 0.8): y'(x) = -0.75\nSlope from explicit formula  d/dx √(1-x²) at x=0.6 =\n   -0.75"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Visualising the implicit function\n\nPlot the implicit curve $F(x, y) = 0$ and overlay the explicit branch $y = +\\sqrt{1 - x^2}$ that the implicit function theorem produces near $(0, 1)$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "theta_g : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 2*float(%pi), 200))$\ncx : map(cos, theta_g)$  cy : map(sin, theta_g)$\nxs : np_to_list(np_linspace(-0.95, 0.95, 200))$\nys_upper : map(lambda([xx], float(sqrt(1 - xx*xx))), xs)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"F(x,y) = x²+y² - 1 = 0\",\n  lines(cx, cy),\n  color=\"red\", line_width=2, name=\"y = +√(1-x²) — local branch\",\n  lines(xs, ys_upper),\n  marker_size=10, color=\"orange\", name=\"critical points (∂F/∂y = 0)\",\n  points([1.0, -1.0], [0.0, 0.0]),\n  title=\"Implicit function theorem — level set is locally a graph\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"y\",\n  grid=true, aspect_ratio=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": [
        {
