85,0.994962152855072,0.9939538121575406,0.9928526361765365,0.9916583156483744,0.9903705135948496,0.9889888648379032,0.9875129754693276,0.9859424222736782,0.9842767521023614,0.982515481196679,0.9806580944573865,0.9787040446581007,0.9766527515996365,0.9745036012020887,0.9722559445311817,0.9699090967550946,0.9674623360276252,0.9649149022931804,0.9622659960086758,0.959514776776972,0.9566603618859926,0.9537018247471255,0.950638193225918,0.9474684478574268,0.9441915199378607,0.9408062894833645,0.9373115830459088,0.9337061713752787,0.9299887669150728,0.9261580211194179,0.9222125215757686,0.91815078891766,0.9139712735096153,0.9096723518845325,0.905252322911777,0.9007094036718403,0.8960417250107715,0.8912473267445787,0.8863241524804113,0.8812700440174757,0.8760827352862799,0.8707598457798221,0.8652988734246632,0.859697186833344,0.8539520168721555,0.8480604474697317,0.8420194055820707,0.8358256502182065,0.8294757604175512,0.8229661220545741,0.8162929133286018,0.8094520887755685,0.8024393616139777,0.7952501842083707,0.7878797263993595,0.7803228514086296,0.7725740889788768,0.7646276053506513,0.7564771696083652,0.7481161158435088,0.7395373004808854,0.7307330539889486,0.721695126042263,0.7124146230152654,0.7028819364519835,0.6930866608633295,0.6830174988348048,0.6726621509599806,0.662007187517889,0.6510378980430136,0.6397381139363734,0.6280899979534408,0.6160737926637694,0.603667517642594,0.5908466019857472,0.5775834343807996,0.5638468068862473,0.549601219951519,0.5348060037810539,0.5194141928750179,0.5033710631447084,0.48661219881394707,0.46906088968207305,0.4506245508134385,0.4311896737330006,0.4106144968748813,0.3887179912550167,0.3652626038599368,0.33992579220092956,0.31224989991991997],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"lines\",\"line\":{\"color\":\"red\",\"width\":2},\"name\":\"y = +√(1-x²) — local branch\"},{\"x\":[1.0,-1.0],\"y\":[0.0,0.0],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"markers\",\"marker\":{\"color\":\"orange\",\"size\":10},\"name\":\"critical points (∂F/∂y = 0)\"}],\"layout\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Implicit function theorem — level set is locally a graph\"},\"showlegend\":true,\"width\":700,\"height\":700,\"plot_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"x\"},\"showgrid\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"y\"},\"showgrid\":true,\"scaleanchor\":\"x\",\"scaleratio\":1,\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A deeper example: solving non-trivial systems\n\nSuppose we have $F : \\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ given by\n\n$$F(x, y, z) = \\begin{pmatrix} x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - 3 \\\\ x + y + z - 3 \\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThe set $\\{F = 0\\}$ is a 1D curve (a circle) in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ — the intersection of a sphere with a plane. The implicit function theorem says: if at a point $p_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on this curve, the partial Jacobian $\\partial F / \\partial(y, z)$ (a $2 \\times 2$ matrix) is invertible, then near $p_0$ we can write $y, z$ as $C^1$ functions of $x$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "F_sys : [x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - 3, x + y + z - 3]$\nJ_full : genmatrix(lambda([i, j], diff(F_sys[i], [x, y, z][j])), 2, 3)$\nprint(\"Full Jacobian J_F (2x3) =\")$ J_full;\n\n/* Take ∂F/∂(y, z) — columns 2 and 3 */\nJ_yz : genmatrix(lambda([i, j], diff(F_sys[i], [y, z][j])), 2, 2)$\nprint(\"∂F/∂(y, z) =\")$ J_yz;\nprint(\"det(∂F/∂(y, z)) =\", ratsimp(determinant(J_yz)))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 18,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Full Jacobian J_F (2x3) =\nmatrix([2*x,2*y,2*z],[1,1,1])\n∂F/∂(y, z) =\nmatrix([2*y,2*z],[1,1])\ndet(∂F/∂(y, z)) = 2*y-2*z"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "$\\det \\partial F/\\partial(y, z) = 2(z - y)$. Non-zero whenever $y \\ne z$. At the point $(1, 0, 2)$: $\\det = 4 \\ne 0$, so we can solve for $(y, z)$ as functions of $x$ near that point. At a symmetric point like $(1, 1, 1)$ (centre of the curve), $\\det = 0$ — there the curve is \"vertical\" in the $x$-direction and $x$ alone can't parametrise.\n\n## Why these theorems matter\n\nBoth are *existence* results — they say \"yes, the function you want is there, even if you can't write it down\". This makes them load-bearing:\n\n- **Numerical solvers.** Newton's method converges quadratically near a root *because* the inverse function theorem says the linearised system is invertible, giving the next iterate.\n- **Optimisation under constraints.** Lagrange multipliers work because the implicit function theorem locally parametrises the constraint surface.\n- **Differential geometry.** A smooth manifold is *defined* as a space that, locally, looks like a graph of one set of coordinates over another — i.e., where the implicit function theorem applies everywhere.\n- **Bifurcation theory.** When $J$ becomes singular, the implicit function theorem fails — and that's where qualitative changes (bifurcations) happen.\n\nYou'll see all four ideas in modern applied analysis. The two theorems are the gateway.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}